sued a universal visual language they called Neoplasticism, built on strict rules:</p>\\n      <ul>\\n        <li><strong>Only horizontal and vertical lines</strong> &mdash; no diagonals, no curves.</li>\\n        <li><strong>Only the three primary colours</strong> &mdash; red, blue and yellow.</li>\\n        <li><strong>Only the non-colours</strong> &mdash; white, grey and black.</li>\\n      </ul>\\n      <p>The rules were not a limitation but a belief: that beneath the chaos of the visible world lay a harmony of straight lines and pure colour, and that art could express it.</p>\\n    </div>\\n  </section>\\n\\n  <section class=\\\"block\\\">\\n    <div class=\\\"side\\\">\\n      <span class=\\\"kicker y\\\">Why it matters</span>\\n      <h2>A reference for modern design</h2>\\n    </div>\\n    <div class=\\\"body\\\">\\n      <p>The painting's power is its asymmetric balance. The huge red square dominates, yet the small blue and yellow blocks hold it in equilibrium &mdash; tension resolved with nothing but placement. A few primary-colour blocks, arranged with total conviction, became a reference for modern graphic design and architecture.</p>\\n      <p>You can trace its logic in magazine layouts, typography, furniture and building facades ever since. Whenever a designer reduces a page to grids, blocks and primary accents, they are speaking Mondrian's language.</p>\\n    </div>\\n  </section>\\n\\n  <div class=\\\"cta-strip\\\">\\n    <h2>See the composition yourself</h2>\\n    <a class=\\\"btn\\\" href=\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Composition_with_Red,_Blue_and_Yellow\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" rel=\\\"noopener\\\">Explore the painting on Wikipedia</a>\\n  </div>\\n\\n  <div class=\\\"source\\\">\\n    Source: <a href=\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Composition_with_Red,_Blue_and_Yellow\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" rel=\\\"noopener\\\">en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Composition_with_Red,_Blue_and_Yellow</a>\\n  </div>\\n\\n  <footer class=\\\"foot\\\" aria-hidden=\\\